<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cædwalla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 659–689</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Cædwalla of Wessex</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cædwalla was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> from about 685 to 688. In a short and violent reign he conquered Sussex, Surrey, Kent and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isle-of-wight">Isle of Wight</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> says he set out to wipe out the native population and had the two young brothers of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arwald">Arwald</a> killed after their baptism. He then gave up his throne, travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and was baptised by Pope Sergius I shortly before his death in 689.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/caedwalla.jpg" alt="Cædwalla" width="320" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lambert Barnard · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:C%C3%A6dwalla_in_Barnard_Chichester_mural_(full_body).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cædwalla was born about 659, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>'s statement that he was about thirty at his death. His name is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> form of the Welsh Cadwallon, and a number of early West Saxon kings had Celtic names. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> makes him the son of Coenberht and a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdic">Cerdic</a>, but later scribes tried to link every king to Cerdic, so the genealogy must be treated with caution. He is first mentioned in the Life of St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid">Wilfrid</a> as an exiled nobleman in the forests of Chiltern and Andred. In 685 he began to contend for the kingdom, defeated and killed Æthelwealh, king of Sussex, and was soon expelled by Æthelwealh's ealdormen Berthun and Andhun.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 685 or 686 Cædwalla became king of the West Saxons after Centwine retired to a monastery. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> says that before his reign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> was ruled by underkings, though this has been questioned. He attacked Sussex again and killed Berthun. He conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isle-of-wight">Isle of Wight</a>, then an independent pagan kingdom. Bede says he set out to kill every native and resettle the island with his own people, though the natives remained a majority. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arwald">Arwald</a>, king of the island, left two young brothers, who were found at Stoneham and killed on his orders after a priest persuaded him to let them be baptised. Cædwalla was wounded in this conquest.</p><p>He controlled Surrey, as a charter of 688 granting land at Farnham for a minster shows, and invaded Kent in 686, installing his brother Mul as king. Mul was burned in a Kentish revolt, according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a>, and Cædwalla returned and laid waste to the land, possibly ruling Kent directly after that. He gave land to churches and, according to the Life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid">Wilfrid</a>, sought out Wilfrid as a spiritual father.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 688 Cædwalla abdicated and set out on pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, perhaps because his wounds were killing him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> says he wished to receive baptism at the shrine of the Apostles. He stopped in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a> at Samer and at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cunipert">Cunincpert</a>, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>. In Rome Pope Sergius I baptised him on the Saturday before Easter, giving him the name Peter, and he died on 20 April 689, still in his white garments. He was buried in St Peter's Basilica, and his epitaph called him King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>. Ine succeeded him, and his departure appears to have led to instability in the south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cædwalla” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under <b>Cædwalla</b> (r. 685–688) the West Saxons conquered the Isle of Wight and Sussex and briefly held Kent, and Ine and his successors pushed into the British kingdom of Dumnonia, taking Devon in the late seventh and eighth centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Caedwalla</b> of Wessex overran Sussex in the 680s, and in the 8th century it fell under Offa of Mercia.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-sussex">Kingdom of Sussex</a></li><li><blockquote>In 686 <b>Caedwalla</b> of the Kingdom of Wessex conquered the island, killed King Arwald and had his two young brothers executed after baptism, as Bede recounts.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-isle-of-wight">Kingdom of the Isle of Wight</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=678" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Cædwalla"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=678">What was here in 678 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
